```c
int deleteRear() {
    if (front == -1) { return -1; } // Empty check
    int val = deque[rear];
    if (front == rear) {
        front = -1;
        rear = -1;
    } else if (rear == 0) {
        rear = N - 1;
    } else {
        rear = rear - 1;
    }
    return val;
}
```

What is the purpose of the condition `if (rear == 0)`?

Analyze the following C snippet for inserting an element at the rear of a circular array deque of size `N`:

```c
void insertRear(int key) {
    if (isFull()) return;
    if (front == -1) {
        front = 0;
        rear = 0;
    } else if (rear == N - 1) {
        rear = 0;
    } else {
        rear = rear + 1;
    }
    deque[rear] = key;
}
```
If `N = 5`, `front = 2`, and `rear = 4`. We call `insertRear(50)`. What will be the new values of `front` and `rear` after the execution?
